    \entry{ShockleyDiodeEquation2025}{inreference}{}{}
      \field{sortinit}{S}
      \field{sortinithash}{b164b07b29984b41daf1e85279fbc5ab}
      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{The Shockley diode equation, or the diode law, named after transistor co-inventor William Shockley of Bell Labs, models the exponential current–voltage (I–V) relationship of semiconductor diodes in moderate constant current forward bias or reverse bias: I D = I S ( e V D n V T − 1 ) , \{\textbackslash displaystyle I\_\{\textbackslash text\{D\}\}=I\_\{\textbackslash text\{S\}\}\textbackslash left(e\textasciicircum\{\textbackslash frac \{V\_\{\textbackslash text\{D\}\}\}\{nV\_\{\textbackslash text\{T\}\}\}\}-1\textbackslash right),\} where I D \{\textbackslash displaystyle I\_\{\textbackslash text\{D\}\}\} is the diode current, I S \{\textbackslash displaystyle I\_\{\textbackslash text\{S\}\}\} is the reverse-bias saturation current (or scale current), V D \{\textbackslash displaystyle V\_\{\textbackslash text\{D\}\}\} is the voltage across the diode, V T \{\textbackslash displaystyle V\_\{\textbackslash text\{T\}\}\} is the thermal voltage, and n \{\textbackslash displaystyle n\} is the ideality factor, also known as the quality factor, emission coefficient, or the material constant. The equation is called the Shockley ideal diode equation when the ideality factor n \{\textbackslash displaystyle n\} equals 1, thus n \{\textbackslash displaystyle n\} is sometimes omitted. The ideality factor typically varies from 1 to 2 (though can in some cases be higher), depending on the fabrication process and semiconductor material. The ideality factor was added to account for imperfect junctions observed in real transistors, mainly due to carrier recombination as charge carriers cross the depletion region. The thermal voltage V T \{\textbackslash displaystyle V\_\{\textbackslash text\{T\}\}\} is defined as: V T = k T q , \{\textbackslash displaystyle V\_\{\textbackslash text\{T\}\}=\{\textbackslash frac \{kT\}\{q\}\},\} where k \{\textbackslash displaystyle k\} is the Boltzmann constant, T \{\textbackslash displaystyle T\} is the absolute temperature of the p–n junction, and q \{\textbackslash displaystyle q\} is the elementary charge (the magnitude of an electron's charge). For example, it is approximately 25.852 mV at 300 K (27 °C; 80 °F). The reverse saturation current I S \{\textbackslash displaystyle I\_\{\textbackslash text\{S\}\}\} is not constant for a given device, but varies with temperature; usually more significantly than V T \{\textbackslash displaystyle V\_\{\textbackslash text\{T\}\}\} , so that V D \{\textbackslash displaystyle V\_\{\textbackslash text\{D\}\}\} typically decreases as T \{\textbackslash displaystyle T\} increases. Under reverse bias, the diode equation's exponential term is near 0, so the current is near the somewhat constant − I S \{\textbackslash displaystyle -I\_\{\textbackslash text\{S\}\}\} reverse current value (roughly a picoampere for silicon diodes or a microampere for germanium diodes, although this is obviously a function of size). For moderate forward bias voltages the exponential becomes much larger than 1, since the thermal voltage is very small in comparison. The − 1 \{\textbackslash displaystyle -1\} in the diode equation is then negligible, so the forward diode current will approximate I S e V D n V T . \{\textbackslash displaystyle I\_\{\textbackslash text\{S\}\}e\textasciicircum\{\textbackslash frac \{V\_\{\textbackslash text\{D\}\}\}\{nV\_\{\textbackslash text\{T\}\}\}\}.\} The use of the diode equation in circuit problems is illustrated in the article on diode modeling.}
